{"time":"2026-07-29T16:14:22.141Z","line":"add","sessionID":"ses_051582d04ffekR1fbwHz1MQoAL","job":"goal","label":"goal: every idle [goal] -> # ROLE You are a senior computational geometry engineer and CFD meshing expert. You have deep experience with: - polyhedral mesh generation - Voronoi / CVT methods - Delaunay refinement - surface remeshing - feature preservation - boundary layer generation - OpenFOAM polyMesh format - robust geometric kernels - C++20, CMake, CGAL, Eigen, TBB, VTK, Python bindings Your task is to design and implement a serious, production-oriented automatic polyhedral mesher, not a toy wrapper around existing meshers. # PROJECT CONTEXT The user has a GUI application that currently automates meshing using cfMesh. It works, but the resulting mesh is not yet a proper base polyhedral mesh as desired. The goal is to create a robust native meshing library/algorithm that can: 1. ingest CAD/surface geometry, 2. repair and prepare it, 3. generate a high-quality 3D polyhedral volume mesh, 4. preserve sharp features, 5. optionally add boundary layers, 6. export solver-ready output, especially OpenFOAM polyMesh, 7. integrate with a GUI through progress/cancel/parameters. # MISSION Create a C++20 library named `autopoly` with optional Python bindings. The library must implement an automatic polyhedral meshing pipeline suitable for CFD preprocessing. Do NOT simply call cfMesh, gmsh, or snappyHexMesh as the core solution. You may use robust geometry libraries and algorithms, but the final pipeline must be a coherent, maintainable, original mesher architecture. # FIRST STEP: INFORMATION GATHERING Before writing code, inspect the repository and identify missing information. If any critical information is missing, output a JSON object like: { \"status\": \"INFO_REQUEST\", \"questions\": [ ... ] } Critical information includes: - target solver/output formats - supported input geometry formats - GUI framework - preferred language and dependencies - licensing constraints - performance targets - whether boundary layers are required - whether STEP/IGES CAD support is required - OS and compiler constraints If enough information is available, proceed with implementation planning. # TECHNICAL STACK Use: - C++20 - CMake - CGAL for robust geometric operations, AABB trees, surface mesh, polygon/polyhedron processing - Eigen for linear algebra - TBB for parallelism - VTK for visualization/export - GTest for unit tests - pybind11 for optional Python bindings - OpenCASCADE only if STEP/IGES support is required Architect the code so CGAL can be isolated behind kernel/geometry adapters. Do not hard-code brittle hacks. Prefer robust, testable modules. # REQUIRED FEATURES Implement the following pipeline: ## 1. Geometry ingestion Support at least: - STL - OBJ - PLY - VTK Optional: - STEP/IGES via OpenCASCADE Requirements: - load triangle soup and/or BREP - merge duplicate vertices - remove degenerate triangles - orient faces consistently - detect and fill small holes if requested - detect self-intersections - detect manifold/non-manifold edges - report geometry health ## 2. Feature extraction and preservation Detect and preserve: - sharp dihedral edges - curvature ridges - user-defined feature edges - small important details Requirements: - feature angle threshold parameter - feature edges must be preserved during surface remeshing and volume snapping - avoid feature smearing ## 3. Size field generation Generate an adaptive target cell size field h(x) based on: - global size - local curvature - proximity to features - thin regions / gap detection - user-defined refinement regions - boundary layer influence Requirements: - smooth size field - avoid abrupt size jumps - support min/max size limits - support grading limits ## 4. Surface preparation and remeshing Generate a clean watertight surface mesh suitable for volume meshing. Requirements: - isotropic or mildly anisotropic triangles - curvature-adaptive sizing - no inverted triangles - no self-intersections - consistent normals - manifold surface - feature vertices/edges constrained - optional surface smoothing - optional surface decimation/refinement ## 5. Volume seed generation Generate 3D seed points inside the domain. Requirements: - seeds from structured jitter grid, Poisson-disk, or CVT initialization - respect local target size h(x) - avoid seeds too close to boundary unless needed - support parallel generation - deterministic behavior with fixed seed ## 6. Polyhedral cell generation Generate a 3D polyhedral mesh from seeds. Preferred robust approach: - compute centroidal Voronoi tessellation (CVT) - use Lloyd relaxation constrained to the domain - clip Voronoi cells against the domain boundary - produce general polyhedral cells Requirements: - cells must be closed 3D polyhedra - no negative volumes - no non-manifold faces - no dangling edges - robust handling of boundary cells - merge coplanar faces where appropriate - collapse tiny edges/faces safely - preserve boundary conformity ## 7. Boundary conformity and snapping Make the volume mesh conform to the input surface. Requirements: - boundary faces must lie on or near the true surface - project boundary vertices to surface - snap feature vertices to feature edges - preserve sharp edges - avoid creating sliver faces - handle concave corners and small gaps robustly ## 8. Boundary layer generation Optional but required if requested. Requirements: - generate prism layers near wall boundaries - support first layer height, growth rate, number of layers - smooth layer normals - avoid self-intersection and layer collision - transition prism-to-polyhedron cleanly - preserve mesh quality in high curvature regions - allow disabling layers on selected patches ## 9. Mesh optimization and quality improvement Implement post-processing optimization. Requirements: - Laplacian smoothing with constraints - feature-preserving smoothing - cell quality optimization - face planarization where useful - removal/repair of bad cells - local refinement/coarsening hooks optional Quality metrics to compute: - cell volume - minimum/maximum face area - non-orthogonality - skewness - aspect ratio / elongation - face warpage - pyramid volume positivity - boundary face validity Default acceptance thresholds: - min cell volume > 1e-30 - max nonOrthogonality < 70 warning, < 85 error - max skewness < 4 warning, < 10 error - no negative volumes - no non-manifold topology - all boundary faces owned by exactly one cell ## 10. Export Support: - OpenFOAM polyMesh export - VTK export - JSON quality/report export OpenFOAM export requirements: - write points, faces, owner, neighbour, boundary - correct face orientation - valid boundary patches - loadable by ParaView and OpenFOAM utilities - if OpenFOAM is available, run `checkMesh` as validation # DATA MODEL Design a clean mesh data structure. Minimum required entities: - points - faces as vertex loops - cells as face lists - boundary patches - feature edges - size field samples - quality report Preferred architecture: - explicit topology with owner/neighbour for volume cells - support arbitrary polygonal faces - support arbitrary polyhedral cells - safe topological edits - validation functions # PUBLIC API Expose a stable high-level API similar to: namespace autopoly { struct Vec3 { double x, y, z; }; struct BoundingBox { Vec3 min; Vec3 max; }; struct GeometryOptions { double mergeTolerance = 1e-9; bool fillSmallHoles = true; double maxHoleArea = -1.0; bool orientNormals = true; bool repairSelfIntersections = false; }; struct FeatureOptions { bool enable = true; double featureAngleDeg = 30.0; double curvatureSensitivity = 0.5; }; struct SizeFieldOptions { double globalSize = 0.05; double minSize = 0.001; double maxSize = 1.0; double curvatureAdaptivity = 0.5; double proximityAdaptivity = 0.5; double maxGrowthRate = 1.2; }; struct SurfaceMeshOptions { double targetSize = 0.03; double minQuality = 0.2; int maxIterations = 20; bool preserveFeatures = true; }; struct VolumeMeshOptions { int seed = 12345; int lloydIterations = 30; double boundaryConformity = 0.9; bool mergeCoplanarFaces = true; double coplanarAngleToleranceDeg = 1.0; double smallFaceAreaTolerance = 1e-12; double minCellVolume = 1e-30; }; struct BoundaryLayerOptions { bool enable = false; int layers = 3; double firstHeight = 0.0005; double growthRate = 1.2; double maxThickness = 0.01; bool smoothNormals = true; bool detectCollisions = true; }; struct QualityOptions { double maxNonOrthoWarn = 70.0; double maxNonOrthoFail = 85.0; double maxSkewnessWarn = 4.0; double maxSkewnessFail = 10.0; double maxAspectRatioWarn = 100.0; double maxAspectRatioFail = 1000.0; }; struct ExportOptions { bool writeVtk = true; bool writeOpenFoam = true; bool writeJsonReport = true; std::string outputDirectory = \"output\"; }; struct ProgressInfo { int stagePercent = 0; std::string stageName; std::string message; bool cancellable = true; }; using ProgressCallback = std::function<void(const ProgressInfo&)>; using CancelToken = std::function<bool()>; struct MeshingParameters { GeometryOptions geometry; FeatureOptions features; SizeFieldOptions sizeField; SurfaceMeshOptions surface; VolumeMeshOptions volume; BoundaryLayerOptions boundaryLayer; QualityOptions quality; ExportOptions exportOptions; }; struct MeshingResult { bool success = false; std::string message; std::string outputDirectory; size_t pointCount = 0; size_t cellCount = 0; size_t faceCount = 0; size_t boundaryPatchCount = 0; double minVolume = 0.0; double maxNonOrthogonality = 0.0; double maxSkewness = 0.0; double maxAspectRatio = 0.0; std::vector<std::string> warnings; std::vector<std::string> errors; }; class Mesher { public: virtual ~Mesher() = default; virtual MeshingResult run( const std::filesystem::path& inputGeometry, const MeshingParameters& params, ProgressCallback progress = {}, CancelToken cancel = {}) = 0; }; std::unique_ptr<Mesher> createMesher(); } // namespace autopoly # GUI INTEGRATION REQUIREMENTS The library must be GUI-friendly. Provide: - progress callbacks by stage - cancellation support - parameter validation - JSON parameter schema - intermediate preview outputs if possible - thread-safe usage guidance - clear error/warning reporting Suggested stages: 1. load geometry 2. repair geometry 3. extract features 4. build size field 5. surface remesh 6. generate seeds 7. CVT / Voronoi 8. boundary clip 9. boundary layer 10. optimization 11. quality check 12. export # ALGORITHMIC EXPECTATIONS Use robust methods, not brittle heuristics. Recommended core approach: 1. Build a watertight, oriented, feature-aware surface mesh. 2. Build an AABB tree and signed distance field / inside-outside oracle. 3. Generate adaptive seeds using the size field. 4. Compute constrained CVT via Lloyd iterations. 5. Build Voronoi cells as half-space intersections. 6. Clip cells against the domain boundary. 7. Extract boundary faces from clipped cells. 8. Snap/project boundary entities to CAD/surface. 9. Merge coplanar faces and simplify small topological artifacts. 10. Optionally extrude boundary layers. 11. Optimize and validate topology/quality. 12. Export. If you choose a different robust approach, justify it and prove it with tests. # PROJECT STRUCTURE Create a clean repository layout: autopoly/ CMakeLists.txt README.md docs/ architecture.md parameters.md validation.md include/autopoly/ Types.h Geometry.h Feature.h SizeField.h SurfaceMesher.h VolumeMesher.h BoundaryLayer.h Quality.h Exporter.h Mesher.h Factory.h src/ Geometry.cpp Feature.cpp SizeField.cpp SurfaceMesher.cpp VolumeMesher.cpp BoundaryLayer.cpp Quality.cpp Exporter.cpp Mesher.cpp Factory.cpp cgal/ CgalKernel.h CgalKernel.cpp apps/ autopoly_cli.cpp python/ autopoly_pybind.cpp tests/ test_geometry.cpp test_surface_mesher.cpp test_volume_mesher.cpp test_quality.cpp test_export.cpp data/ cube.stl sphere.stl cylinder.stl broken_cube.stl thin_gap.stl sharp_feature.stl # TESTING REQUIREMENTS Add automated tests for: ## Unit tests, max no-progress 3"}
